Consider a relativistic collision system that produces a small number of final-state particles, such as a proton–proton or proton–nucleus collision at LHC energies. Standard relativistic viscous hydrodynamics is expected to be valid only when (i) the ratio of the microscopic mean free path λ to the characteristic system size L satisfies λ/L ≪ 1, and (ii) the average number of secondary interactions experienced by a typical constituent during the lifetime of the system is large (≫ 1). Empirically, however, hydrodynamic simulations applied to such small and dilute systems reproduce observed azimuthal anisotropy coefficients v_n, identified-particle spectra, and other collective observables to a degree comparable with their success in large nucleus–nucleus collisions, even though the conditions (i)–(ii) are apparently not satisfied.

Provide a quantitative dynamical explanation, derived from an underlying microscopic theory of QCD or from a controlled effective description, that accounts for why a hydrodynamic (or hydrodynamic-like) description yields the observed collective behavior in these small dilute systems. The explanation should specify:
(a) the mechanism by which approximate local equilibration or "hydrodynamization" is reached on the relevant short timescales, if any equilibration is achieved at all;
(b) the regime of validity of the effective hydrodynamic description in terms of λ/L, opacity, or analogous parameters;
(c) the matching between the pre-equilibrium dynamics and the hydrodynamic stage in systems with very few scatterings per particle;
(d) testable predictions distinguishing genuine hydrodynamic flow from alternative origins (e.g., initial-state momentum correlations of color-glass-condensate type or kinetic-theory few-scattering effects).

